This view is a complete replacement for ContentView.swift in an Xcode SwiftUI app project. Keep the generated App file that provides the app's @main entry point, and run in a compatible Apple simulator or device.

```swift
import SwiftUI

struct ContentView: View {
    @State private var name = ""

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            TextField("Your nickname", text: $name)
                .textFieldStyle(.roundedBorder)
            Text("Hello, \(name)!")
        }
        .padding()
    }
}
```

Typing changes name and refreshes the greeting. import loads the SwiftUI module's types. : View declares protocol conformance. body is a computed property that describes the screen. some View means its result is a particular view type that the definition need not spell out.

This is another complete ContentView.swift replacement. Run it as a separate example in the same kind of Xcode project.

```swift
import SwiftUI

struct ContentView: View {
    @State private var draft = ""
    @State private var observations: [String] = []

    var body: some View {
        NavigationStack {
            VStack {
                TextField("What did you notice?", text: $draft)
                    .textFieldStyle(.roundedBorder)
                Button("Add observation") {
                    let cleaned = draft.trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespacesAndNewlines)
                    guard !cleaned.isEmpty else { return }
                    observations.append(cleaned)
                    draft = ""
                }
                List(observations.indices, id: \.self) { index in
                    Text(observations[index])
                }
            }
            .padding()
            .navigationTitle("Observations")
        }
    }
}
```

The Button action is a closure that runs on activation. It cleans input, rejects a blank result, appends one observation, and clears the field. List turns the current entries into rows.

